<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  What this build is allowed to do.

  Not "ignored until somebody signs one", which is what this file used to say and what made the
  macOS build unusable for four releases. Tauri signs the bundle ad-hoc (`signingIdentity: "-"`)
  and it signs it with the *hardened runtime*, so these entitlements are embedded in every copy
  that ships and they are enforced.

  Deliberately short. An app that promises audio never leaves the machine should not be asking for
  the network, the camera, or a user's documents.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <!--
      The microphone. The hardened runtime denies audio input by default, so without this a signed
      build installs, opens, and cannot record.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      The daemon has to be allowed to load the two libraries shipped beside it, and under the
      hardened runtime it is not allowed to by default.

      Library validation says a process may load only libraries signed by the *same team* as
      itself, or by Apple. An ad-hoc signature has no team, so "the same team" matches nothing that
      is not part of macOS — and `Contents/Resources/lib` holds ONNX Runtime and sherpa-onnx, which
      are neither. The result on a user's machine was total, and it named a library rather than a
      policy:

        dyld[16202]: Library not loaded: @rpath/libonnxruntime.1.17.1.dylib
          Reason: … code signature in '…/Contents/Resources/lib/libonnxruntime.1.17.1.dylib'
          not valid for use in process: mapping process and mapped file (non-platform) have
          different Team IDs

      Both files are ours: staged by `scripts/sidecar.sh` from the build, ad-hoc signed there, and
      sealed into the bundle. The check being switched off is one that could only ever have said no
      to them.

      This stays true of a notarised build. Apple allows this exception under a Developer ID, and
      Tauri does not sign what is under `Resources/`, so a certificate would leave the libraries
      carrying our ad-hoc signature and the same rule would refuse them for the same reason.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
